# Binary assets must never go through end-of-line conversion.
#
# Added after a real corruption: on a Windows checkout, git's autocrlf
# rewrote every 0x0A inside public/winner-testimonial-sample-scripts.pdf as
# 0x0D 0x0A. That shifted the file by ~2KB, so every byte offset in the
# PDF's own cross-reference table pointed at the wrong place and the file
# would not open. The blob in the repository was fine; only the working
# copy was destroyed, which is exactly the kind of failure that gets
# noticed late.
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
*.mp3 binary
*.wav binary

# Shell scripts must always check out with LF, never CRLF. A CRLF right
# after the #!/usr/bin/env bash shebang line makes the interpreter line
# unparsable on a real Linux/macOS shell (the shebang becomes
# "#!/usr/bin/env bash\r", which most kernels fail to resolve) -- the same
# class of corruption the PDF rule above exists for, just for text instead
# of bytes. It happened to run fine under Git Bash on Windows in this repo
# because that build tolerates a trailing \r, which is exactly what let a
# CRLF checkout go unnoticed here before it broke somewhere stricter.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning as the shell-script rule above, applied preemptively rather
# than after a real break: Postgres itself doesn't care about CRLF vs LF in
# a .sql file, but 122 migrations checked out consistently as LF and one new
# one silently flipping to CRLF on a Windows checkout is exactly the kind of
# quiet inconsistency that's cheap to prevent and annoying to debug later.
*.sql text eol=lf
